  "confidence": 0.97,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["Q1", "Q2", "R1", "R2"],
      "pins": ["Q1.1", "Q2.1", "Q2.3", "R1.2", "R2.1", "R2.2", "U2.6", "U3.6"],
      "nets": ["PWM", "Net-(Q2-B)"],
      "observed": "PWM directly joins the MCU PWM pins, Q1's PMOS gate, and Q2's collector. R2 connects that same PWM net to Q2's base, while Q2's emitter is on GND. Thus a high PWM output also drives Q2 on, causing it to pull the PWM/gate node toward ground.",
      "expected": "A transistor used to pull the PMOS gate low should have its collector on the gate node and its base driven by a separate control signal through a resistor; the MCU output should not be directly tied to the collector node that the transistor pulls low.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "PWM : ... Q1.1(G,input) Q2.3(C,passive) ... U2.6(PB1,bidirectional) U3.6(PB1,bidirectional)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "Net-(Q2-B) : Q2.1(B,input) R2.2(passive)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "R2 | 1K | Resistor_SMD:R_0805_2012Metric"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "GND : ... Q2.2(E,passive) ..."
        }
      ],
      "impact": "Q2 can clamp the MCU PWM signal and PMOS gate when the signal is driven high, causing distorted or ineffective PWM and potentially forcing the fan power switch into unintended states.",
      "confidence": 0.97
    },
    {
      "category": "extra_component",
      "components": ["U2", "U3"],
      "pins": ["U2.1", "U2.2", "U2.3", "U2.4", "U2.5", "U2.6", "U2.7", "U2.8", "U3.1", "U3.2", "U3.3", "U3.4", "U3.5", "U3.6", "U3.7", "U3.8"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "GND", "POT", "PWM", "RST", "SCK", "SDA", "TACH"],
      "observed": "The ATtiny45 (U2) and ATtiny13V (U3) are both connected pin-for-pin to the same supply, ground, reset, and signal nets, including the PWM output pin.",
      "expected": "A fan controller should use one active MCU, or provide a means to select or isolate alternative MCUs. Two MCUs connected in parallel can contend on shared signals.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "U2 | ATtiny45-20P | Package_DIP:DIP-8_W7.62mm"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "U3 | ATtiny13V-10S | Package_SO:SOIC-8W_5.3x5.3mm_P1.27mm"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "PWM : ... U2.6(PB1,bidirectional) U3.6(PB1,bidirectional)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "SDA : ... U2.5(AREF/PB0,bidirectional) U3.5(PB0,bidirectional)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "+5V : ... U2.8(VCC,power_in) U3.8(VCC,power_in)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If both MCUs are populated, their outputs may conflict and their shared reset and signal lines may prevent reliable operation. If one is intended as an alternate assembly option, the files show no selection or isolation mechanism.",
      "confidence": 0.84
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The PWM net appears to make Q2 clamp the MCU output and PMOS gate, a clear functional concern. The two different MCUs are also connected in parallel on all controller nets, which risks contention if both are assembled."
}

